      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two positive real numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that their geometric mean is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and their harmonic mean is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nGiven:\n1. Geometric mean: \\(\\sqrt{xy} = \\sqrt{3}\\)\n2. Harmonic mean: \\(\\frac{2xy}{x + y} = \\frac{3}{2}\\)\n\nLet's start by solving the geometric mean equation:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{xy} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nxy = 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve the harmonic mean equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x + y} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( xy = 3 \\) into the equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{x + y} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{x + y} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n6 \\cdot 2 = 3 \\cdot (x + y)\n\\]\n\\[\n12 = 3(x + y)\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\nx + y = 4\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n1. \\( xy = 3 \\)\n2. \\( x + y = 4 \\)\n\nThese equations represent a quadratic equation whose roots are \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We can form the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nt^2 - (x + y)t + xy = 0\n\\]\nSubstituting the values \\( x + y = 4 \\) and \\( xy = 3 \\), we get:\n\\[\nt^2 - 4t + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -4 \\), and \\( c = 3 \\):\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 + 2}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 - 2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3, 1}\n\\]",
